Two threads can both pass the balance check before either subtracts, so the account goes negative.

```java
public class BankAccount {
    private int balance = 1000;

    // UNSAFE: two threads can interleave the check and the update
    public void withdraw(int amount) {
        if (balance >= amount) {
            balance -= amount; // both threads may pass the check first
        }
    }
}
```

```java
public class BankAccount {
    private int balance = 1000;

    // SAFE: only one thread runs withdraw on this account at a time
    public synchronized void withdraw(int amount) {
        if (balance >= amount) {
            balance -= amount; // critical section is protected
        }
    }
}
```
*The intrinsic lock on the account serializes every withdraw, so the check and the update happen as one atomic step.*

`count++` compiles to read, add, and write. Two threads interleave those steps and lose updates.

```java
public class VisitorCounter {
    private int count = 0;

    // UNSAFE: count++ is read-modify-write, threads interleave
    public void increment() {
        count++; // three steps: read, add, write
    }

    public int get() { return count; }
}
```

```java
public class VisitorCounter {
    private int count = 0;

    // SAFE: synchronized makes the whole increment atomic
    public synchronized void increment() {
        count++; // only one thread at a time
    }

    public synchronized int get() { return count; }
}
```
*With both methods synchronized, two threads each incrementing 1000 times always land on exactly 2000.*

```java
public class Counter {
    private int count;
    private final Object lock = new Object(); // dedicated private monitor

    // Synchronized method: locks the entire 'this' object for the whole method
    public synchronized void incMethod() {
        count++;
    }

    // Synchronized block: only the critical line is serialized
    public void incBlock() {
        prepare();                  // runs concurrently — not under the lock
        synchronized (lock) {
            count++;                // narrow critical section = less contention
        }
    }

    private void prepare() { /* expensive, thread-safe prep */ }
}
```

Locking on a private final object (not on this) stops external code from accidentally locking the same monitor.

```java
// Every increment acquires the monitor, so threads serialize and block
public class SyncCounter {
    private int count = 0;
    public synchronized void increment() { count++; }
    public synchronized int get() { return count; }
}
```

```java
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

// CAS-based: no blocking, scales better for a single shared counter
public class AtomicCounter {
    private final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger();
    public void increment() { count.incrementAndGet(); } // atomic, non-blocking
    public int get() { return count.get(); }
}
```

Use AtomicInteger for one independent variable. Reach for synchronized when several fields must change together as one invariant.

When the lock order follows the argument order, two opposite transfers can wait on each other forever.

```java
public class BankAccount {
    private final long id;
    private int balance;

    public BankAccount(long id, int balance) {
        this.id = id;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    // DEADLOCK PRONE: lock order varies with the call
    public void transfer(BankAccount to, int amount) {
        synchronized (this) {        // locks source first
            synchronized (to) {      // then destination
                this.balance -= amount;
                to.balance += amount;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```java
    // DEADLOCK SAFE: one consistent global lock order
    public void transfer(BankAccount to, int amount) {
        BankAccount first  = this.id < to.id ? this : to;
        BankAccount second = this.id < to.id ? to : this;
        synchronized (first) {
            synchronized (second) {
                this.balance -= amount;
                to.balance += amount;
            }
        }
    }
```
*A consistent lock order means no two threads can ever wait on each other in a cycle.*

Without a memory barrier, the reader may spin on a stale `false` and never see the writer's update.

```java
public class VisibilityDemo {
    private boolean ready = false; // not volatile
    private int value = 0;

    public void writer() {
        value = 42;
        ready = true; // may stay in this core's cache
    }

    public void reader() {
        while (!ready) { }          // may spin forever on a stale false
        System.out.println(value);  // may even print 0
    }
}
```

```java
public class VisibilityDemo {
    private boolean ready = false;
    private int value = 0;
    private final Object lock = new Object();

    public void writer() {
        synchronized (lock) {   // flushes writes on release
            value = 42;
            ready = true;
        }
    }

    public void reader() {
        synchronized (lock) {   // refreshes cache on acquire
            while (!ready) { }
            System.out.println(value); // guaranteed to see 42
        }
    }
}
```
*Entering and leaving the same lock builds a happens-before edge, so the reader always sees the writer's latest values.*
